{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>On the other side of the Jordan, in the land of Moab, Moses undertook to explain.</b> And since they would journey no more — for in the plains of Moab was the final encampment of their marches — and since he had already pleaded and despaired of crossing over, he undertook to explain whatever he thought might fall into doubt after his death. He began the explanation by saying afterward, ה׳ אֱלֹהֵינוּ כָּרַת עִמָּנוּ בְּרִית בְּחוֹרֵב לֵאמֹר (Deuteronomy 5:2). And before beginning the explanation in which he elaborated many warnings concerning certain commandments, he said to Israel that the reason this explanation and these warnings were now necessary was that they were about to enter the land without him, and he would be unable to warn them at the time of action or to resolve every doubt that might arise. And he recounted how they stumbled in this matter — that he would not cross over with them — through their own free choice and their transgressions, so that they might guard against this henceforth, in order not to bring ruin upon their affairs."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:20:04.913760109Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}